This probably doesn't help in this case, but I remember when playing on 
machines with no virtual memory such things often happened to me - basically
it was that NETHACK saved the memory pointers to various strings and the
next time got loaded at different address so it got everything mixed up. 
The cure was to ensure that the environment was the same each game (i.e.
watch where the program was loaded). Now IBM/RISC 6000 should be a virtual 
memory machine and not have this problem - but the first three letters of
the name are giving me trouble... ;)  Maybe you system was reconfigured
and the layout of the virtual address space got changed? Anyhow - don't 
throw away the save file yet - maybe you manage to get it working again...
